ometry of antimatroidal point sets (https://arxiv.org/abs/0806.2096)\nciteturn1academia14 [wordlim: 200] Published: 18.4 years ago; Title: Geometry of antimatroidal point sets ... When the underlying set consists of only two elements, such two-dimensional antimatroids correspond to point sets in the plane.\nTitle: Geometry of antimatroidal point sets\nAuthors: Yulia Kempner, Vadim E. Levit\nDate: Thu Jun 12 15:24:30 2008\n\nThe notion of \"antimatroid with repetition\" was conceived by Bjorner, Lovasz and Shor in 1991 as a multiset extension of the notion of antimatroid. When the underlying set consists of only two elements, such two-dimensional antimatroids correspond to point sets in the plane. In this research we concentrate on geometrical properties of antimatroidal point sets in the plane and prove that these sets are exactly parallelogram polyominoes. Our results imply that two-dimensional antimatroids have convex dimension 2. The second part of the research is devoted to geometrical properties of three-dimensional antimatroids closed under intersection.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nGeometry of antimatroidal point sets (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/1736265_Geometry_of_antimatroidal_point_sets)\nciteturn1search2 [wordlim: 200] Published: 18.4 years ago; Crawled: 5 months ago; Lemma 2.2 implies that every antimatroidal point set is a union of rect- ... Theorem 2.3 A set of points S in the digital plane Z 2 is an antimatroidal ... Thus, an n-step staircase C is accessible.\nLemma 2.2 implies that every antimatroidal point set is a union of rect-\n\nangles built on each pair of non-comparable points. The following theorem\n\nshows that antimatroidal point sets in the plane and parallelogram polyomi-\n\nnoes are equivalent.\n\nTheorem 2.3 A set of points S in the digital plane Z 2 is an antimatroidal\n\npoint set if and only if it is an orthogonally convex N 4-connected set that is\n\nbounded by 